  "1dc363a4-0979-4fba-bed0-49ed7716b12b"],
 "\nTo encode the state graph, it suffices to record the arc labels \
associated with each boundary component of the surface.  For example, \
starting at the arc labeled 1, and tracing the component until it closes up \
one passes through arcs labeled 7 and 10 as well.  This component is labeled \
arc[1,7,10].  \n\nThe interactions, indicated by a diamond in the figure, are \
encoded with V[a,b] where a and b each correspond to the minimum labels on \
their respective components.  For the example we obtain the following code:\
\[LineSeparator]\nK4State = \
arc[1,7,10]arc[3,5,1]arc[2,9,6]arc[4,8,12]V[1,2]V[1,3]V[1,4]V[2,3]V[2,4]V[3,4]\
.\n\nNote that if a virtual crossing of the form V[a,a] appears in the code \
for a state, then the state cannot be colored with any number of colors.\n\n\
For our calculations we will work with a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]capped\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] configuration.   For example, suppose a graph \
contains Birkhoff diamond.  One may resolve all edges away from the diamond \
and its spokes by choosing a clasp or a buckle. After removing any circles \
that do not interact with the configuration, one is left with a capped \
configuration.  One example is shown below.\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
