  "8c7e8f8d-3e2b-4fae-8d7e-b3714bd7c100"],
 "\nFor the capped configuration we decompose it into two parts:  1) the cap \
and 2) the configuration.  \n1) For the cap, we encode a family of arcs and \
the interactions.  Arcs are labeled by their endpoints as arc[a,b].  For an \
interaction, we choose the smaller of the two arc labels for each arc and \
denote the interaction with V[a,b]. For the example above we obtain:\nCap := \
arc[1,12]arc[2,11]arc[3,8]arc[4,7]arc[5,10]arc[6,9]V[2,3]V[3,5]V[3,6]V[4,5]V[\
4,6]\n2) For the Birkhoff diamond configuration we record the perfect \
matching edges using the same convention described above:\n\n\
BirkhoffDiamond:=M[34,36,13,15]M[28,30,16,18]M[32,31,23,22]M[46,48,26,25]M[30,\
29,33,32]M[39,38,29,28]M[43,45,31,33]M[12,11,35,34]M[36,35,37,39]M[40,42,38,\
37]M[42,41,44,43]M[45,44,47,46]M[48,47,8,7]M[10,9,41,40]M[15,14,2,1]M[14,13,\
18,17]M[17,16,19,21]M[21,20,4,3]M[20,19,22,24]M[24,23,25,27]M[27,26,6,5].\n\n\
The advantage of separating the cap in this way is that we can now \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]plug in\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] different \
configurations to the cap.  For example, we can replace the Birkhoff diamond \
with reducer shown below:\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
